A length-one atomic vector is a single value, not an array, unless it is
wrapped in \code{\link[=I]{I()}} or \code{auto_unbox = FALSE}. A matrix is a flat array in
column-major order. An object whose class zucbor does not know goes
through \code{\link[=as_cbor]{as_cbor()}} first, so a method can say how to write it; without
one it is written as its underlying type.

Whole doubles become integers because R has no integer literal: \code{-7}
written in R is a double, and as a CBOR float it would be a different
value from the integer a COSE algorithm identifier needs. So \code{1L} and \code{1}
encode identically.

These have no CBOR form and raise \code{zucbor_unsupported_type}: complex
numbers, functions, environments, external pointers, S4 objects,
\code{POSIXlt} (convert with \code{\link[=as.POSIXct]{as.POSIXct()}}), and data frame columns that are
matrices, data frames or raw vectors. Names that are partly missing, \code{NA}
or empty are \code{zucbor_invalid_argument}, and two keys that encode
identically are \code{zucbor_duplicate_key}.

A \code{\link[=cbor_tag]{cbor_tag()}} is written as given, but its content must be what
\code{\link[=cbor_validate]{cbor_validate()}} accepts under that tag number: text under tag 0 must be
an RFC 3339 date/time, a typed array a whole number of elements, and so
on; anything else is \code{zucbor_invalid_argument}. Under a tag whose
content cannot be an array, a length-one vector is one value even with
\code{auto_unbox = FALSE}. Tags 2 and 3 are written
in preferred form, without leading zero bytes, and as a plain integer
when the value fits in 64 bits.
}

\section{Data frames}{

A data frame is written row by row, as \code{zujson} writes one: an array of
maps, each keyed by the column names, so \code{data.frame(a = 1:2)} is
\verb{[\{"a": 1\}, \{"a": 2\}]}. A cell is written as the same element of its
column would be in a vector, \code{NA} as \code{null}, and a list column's cell as
a value of its own. Row names are dropped. A column of a class zucbor
does not know goes through \code{\link[=as_cbor]{as_cbor()}} whole, once. \code{cbor_decode(x, data_frame = TRUE)} reads such an array back as a data frame.
}

\section{Typed arrays}{

With \code{typed_arrays = TRUE}, an \code{integer} or \code{double} vector that would be
written as an array (not a single value) is one tagged byte string
instead of one item per element: tag 78 (32-bit signed integers) or tag
86 (64-bit floats), little-endian on every platform. Every bit is kept:
\code{NA_integer_} is written as the smallest 32-bit integer, which decodes to
\code{NA_integer_} again, and \code{NA_real_}, \code{NaN} and \code{-0} keep their bits. A
whole double stays a float, unlike in an array. A matrix or array, of any
type, is tag 1040: its dimensions and its elements in R's column-major
order, the elements a typed array when they are numeric. Vectors with a
class, such as factors and dates, are written as without the option.

For 10^6 doubles, a typed array is 8 MB against 9 MB as an array, and is
written and read several times faster.
}

\section{Conversions that do not round-trip}{

Decoding what \code{cbor_encode()} wrote gives back the value, except that:
\code{NA} comes back as \code{NULL}, or \code{NA} of the vector's type; a whole double
comes back as an integer; \code{list(1L)} and \code{1L} both encode as \code{1};
\code{NaN} payloads are not kept; fractional days of a \code{Date} are dropped; and
a data frame comes back, with \code{data_frame = TRUE}, without its row names,
with factors as text, and with its columns in the encoded key order; a
\code{POSIXct} comes back in UTC, whatever its \code{tzone}; attributes other than
names, \code{dim} and class are dropped; \code{cbor_tag(55799, v)} comes back as
\code{v}; and a \code{cbor_bigint}, or a tag 2 or 3, whose value fits in 64 bits
comes back as an integer or a double.
}

